    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail for a boring reason: they are treated as promises when they are actually guesses. A roadmap is a set of bets about what customers will want, what engineers can build, and what the market will reward. Bets are supposed to be revisited. But the moment a roadmap gets shown to a sales team or a board, it stops being a bet and becomes a contract. From then on, the goal quietly shifts from learning what to build to defending what was already written down.\n\nThat shift explains most of the downstream dysfunction. Once the roadmap is a contract, changing it looks like failure, so teams ship features that no longer make sense rather than admit the assumption underneath them collapsed two months ago. Nobody wants to be the person who tells the CRO that the integration promised in Q3 turned out to serve eleven customers. So it gets built, badly, and everyone moves on.\n\nThe second common failure is that roadmaps list outputs instead of outcomes. A row that says \"notifications redesign\" tells you nothing about what problem is being solved or how you would know if it worked. Teams then optimize for shipping the thing on the date, because that is the only measurable commitment in the room. You end up with a product that is technically on schedule and strategically adrift. When roadmaps are framed around problems, like reducing the number of users who abandon setup, teams retain the freedom to solve them in whatever way actually works.\n\nThird, roadmaps usually lie about capacity. They are drawn as if all engineering time is available for new features, when in practice a large share disappears into on-call, security patches, migrations, flaky tests, and the accumulated cost of last year's shortcuts. A roadmap that ignores this is not a plan, it is a wish. The predictable result is slipping dates, which erodes trust, which produces pressure for more detailed roadmaps, which slip further.\n\nFourth, many roadmaps are assembled politically rather than analytically. The items on them reflect who asked loudest: the biggest account, the founder's recent conversation at a conference, the executive who owns the quarterly review. This produces a list that is individually defensible and collectively incoherent. Nothing compounds, because each item serves a different theory of the business.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps have no expiry date and no maintenance. Someone builds a beautiful artifact, presents it, and then it lives in a slide deck while the real work is coordinated in tickets and Slack threads. Within six weeks the roadmap and reality have diverged, and everyone has learned to ignore it.\n\nThe fix is not better software or prettier timelines. It is being honest about confidence. Near term work can be specific because you know a lot. Anything past a quarter or two should be expressed as themes and open questions, with the explicit understanding that it will change as you learn. Roadmaps fail when they pretend to certainty nobody has. They work when they make the uncertainty visible and give teams permission to act on what they discover.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they make uncertain work look certain. A row of features arranged neatly across four quarters suggests that a team knows what customers will need, how long development will take, and what the business should prioritize months from now. Usually, it knows much less than the roadmap implies.\n\nThe trouble often starts with the question the roadmap is meant to answer. Instead of asking which customer problems matter most, teams ask what they can promise to deliver. Sales wants a feature that might close a deal. Leadership wants something impressive for the next board meeting. Existing customers want their requests acknowledged. The roadmap becomes a negotiated settlement between stakeholders rather than a coherent plan for improving the product.\n\nOnce those promises acquire dates, changing direction gets harder. A team may discover that customers do not care about a planned feature, or that a simpler solution would work better. But removing the feature now looks like breaking a commitment. People keep building because the roadmap says they should, even when the original reason for building has disappeared. The plan survives at the expense of its purpose.\n\nRoadmaps also tend to confuse shipping with success. Launching a dashboard is easy to recognize as a completed task. Helping customers make better decisions is harder to measure and may require several attempts. When delivery is the main measure of progress, teams have an incentive to finish what they listed, not to investigate whether it helped. A roadmap can be completed on schedule while the product barely improves.\n\nThen there is the work that never makes it onto the slide: maintenance, security fixes, support, hiring, technical dependencies, and the unexpected complexity of changing an established system. Plans built around uninterrupted development are already behind before anyone starts. Adding more detail rarely fixes this. It can simply make unrealistic assumptions look more convincing.\n\nA useful roadmap does not eliminate uncertainty. It makes uncertainty visible. It explains which problems deserve attention, why they matter, and what evidence would justify changing course. Near-term commitments can be specific, while later work remains provisional. It leaves room for the less glamorous work that keeps a product reliable.\n\nThe real test of a roadmap is not whether the team follows it exactly. It is whether it helps people make better decisions as they learn. Most roadmaps fail when they stop being tools for those decisions and become promises nobody feels allowed to question.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
